<!--
  bb-isolated — the libvirt network a blastbox VM worker attaches to by default.

  THE ABSENCE OF <forward> IS THE WHOLE POINT. That is libvirt's isolated mode: guests on
  this network reach each other and the host, and nothing is forwarded to the physical
  NIC. There is no rule to get wrong and no daemon to fail open — a packet has nowhere to
  go because no path exists, which is the same property the container tier gets from
  docker's `internal` bridge flag.

  DO NOT ADD <forward mode='nat'/>. libvirt's shipped `default` network has exactly that,
  and VmConfig.network used to point at it: a worker with no egress_policy therefore had
  working internet, from the defaults alone, with no host-side rules. VmRuntime refuses
  to boot that pairing now (see _assert_egress_is_governed), and this file is the other
  half of the fix.

  A worker that NEEDS egress gets it the governed way — an egress_policy applied to its
  IP by the rooter (blastbox.host.runtime.libvirt_egress) — not by being placed on a
  network that forwards.

  DHCP is offered because the guest agent is reached over this network and the IP
  reservation scheme (net-update add ip-dhcp-host) writes into it. The range is the
  tier's, not libvirt's default 192.168.122.0/24, so defining this cannot disturb an
  existing `default` network on the same host.

  Install:
      virsh net-define deploy/libvirt/bb-isolated.xml
      virsh net-start bb-isolated
      virsh net-autostart bb-isolated
  VERIFIED ON REAL LIBVIRT (genesis, libvirt 11.1.0, 2026-09-17). Defined, started, and
  probed with a veth in a network namespace attached to the bridge, which is what a
  guest's tap is topologically:
      bridge address 192.168.221.1/24, namespace at .50
      ping 192.168.221.1  -> YES     (the segment is LIVE; a negative below means something)
      curl https://1.1.1.1 -> no answer
      ping 1.1.1.1         -> unreachable
  With a CONTROL, because "no internet" is worthless if the probe cannot detect internet:
  the identical probe in a routed+masqueraded namespace on the same host reached
  1.1.1.1 and reported the site's egress address. So the probe works, and this network
  does not carry it.

  MEASURED ON genesis, libvirt 11.1.0, with the agent path checked first every time:

      A -> host bridge              YES     the guest agent must stay reachable
      A -> sibling worker B         blocked  <- <port isolated='yes'/>
      A -> internet                 blocked  <- no <forward> element
      dnsmasq conf has port=0       yes      <- <dns enable='no'/>
      listening on 192.168.221.1:53 nothing
      dig from the guest            exit 9 (no server)
      DHCP range still offered      yes

  HOW THE ISOLATION IS ENFORCED, measured rather than assumed (genesis, libvirt 11.1.0):
  libvirt installs NO FORWARD RULES AT ALL for this bridge. `iptables -S FORWARD | grep
  bb-virbr0` is empty. Isolation here is the ABSENCE of a path, not the presence of a
  DROP — which is the same property the container tier gets from an internal bridge, and
  the reason a dead tunnel is a dead route rather than a rule someone can delete.

  THAT HAS ONE CONSEQUENCE WORTH STATING, because a reader will otherwise assume a DROP:
  anything that SUPPLIES a path gets traffic out. Verified on the same host — a worker on
  this network has no internet, and after the rooter's host-side source routing is applied
  (`ip rule from <worker> lookup <table>` + a default in that table + MASQUERADE) the same
  worker reaches the internet. That is BY DESIGN: the rooter is the governed path, and it
  is how a tunnel-tier worker (openvpn/wireguard) reaches its endpoint from here. A review
  round suspected the isolated default broke those workers; it does not.

  It also means bb-isolated is not a defence against a stray host-side rule. It is the
  default-deny floor; the FORWARD chains in blastbox.host.runtime.libvirt_egress are what
  police a worker that has been given a path.

  Verify it does NOT forward:
      virsh net-dumpxml bb-isolated | grep -c '<forward'     # must print 0
-->
<network>
  <name>bb-isolated</name>
  <!--
    isolated='yes' — GUESTS CANNOT TALK TO EACH OTHER. Without it every worker on this
    bridge can reach every other worker: a warm pool of analysis VMs is a flat L2 segment,
    and a sample that moves laterally attacks its siblings, corrupting their verdicts as
    well as escaping its own box. Omitting <forward> stops traffic leaving the HOST; this
    stops it crossing between guests, which is a different question and was unanswered.
    Guest-to-host still works, so the agent stays reachable. Needs libvirt >= 6.1.
  -->
  <bridge name='bb-virbr0' stp='on' delay='0'/>
  <port isolated='yes'/>
  <!--
    DNS OFF. libvirt's generated conf for an isolated network happens to carry `no-resolv`,
    so its dnsmasq has no upstream servers and external names return NO ANSWER — verified
    on genesis. But that is libvirt's generation CHOICE, not a property of this file, and
    this session's repeated lesson is not to rest a containment claim on another
    component's default. Disabling the DNS service makes it structural: there is no
    resolver on the segment to forward anything, so the classic TXT/NXDOMAIN exfiltration
    channel is absent by construction rather than by configuration. DHCP is unaffected —
    it is a separate service in the same dnsmasq.
  -->
  <dns enable='no'/>
  <ip address='192.168.221.1' netmask='255.255.255.0'>
    <dhcp>
      <range start='192.168.221.2' end='192.168.221.254'/>
    </dhcp>
  </ip>
</network>
